<TopicPage slug="subjects/massage-therapy/foundational/body-systems-and-movement">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Massage therapy is structured touch applied with anatomical intent. Its foundation is the
    body-sciences trio — anatomy and physiology, kinesiology, and the pathology that decides what
    is safe — because "massage the back" is not a treatment until it names a muscle, a direction,
    a pressure and a reason.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="systems, muscles, movement language — and pressure" widget="equationStep" config={foundationsCfg} />

  <h2>Pressure is physics you control</h2>
  <p>
    Pressure = force ÷ contact area. The same 40 N feels firm through a forearm, brutal through a
    thumb, and gentle through a broad palm. Professional technique is largely the deliberate
    choice of contact surface — and the reason therapists save their thumbs by using body weight
    rather than finger strength. Drag the sliders and feel the arithmetic.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="force ÷ area = the pressure the tissue feels" widget="sliderLab" config={kinesCfg} />

  <h2>Kinesiology makes assessment honest</h2>
  <p>
    "Shoulder pain" becomes workable in movement terms: which motion hurts (abduction? rotation?),
    at what range, against what resistance? The MBLEx tests this vocabulary because treatment
    plans live in it — a session is planned as "reduce restriction in medial rotation" not "relax
    the shoulder", and improvement is measured against the same words.
  </p>

  <h2>Pathology decides the plan</h2>
  <p>
    The sciences also draw the safety map: acute inflammation, deep-vein thrombosis, some skin
    infections and certain cardiovascular conditions change or cancel treatment. Knowing the
    body systems is therefore two skills in one — where to work, and when to stop and refer.
    Both are examined, and both are the licence's real content.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FSMTB MBLEx content outline; Fritz, <em>Mosby's Fundamentals of Therapeutic Massage</em>; Salvo, <em>Massage Therapy: Principles and Practice</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
